# Building Pncad (OpenSCAD Viewer for Android)

This document describes how to set up the development environment, install
dependencies, and build the application from source.

## Prerequisites

- JDK 17+
- Android SDK (API level 34)
- Android NDK r25+ (r27 recommended)
- CMake 3.22+  (installed via Android SDK Manager)
- Gradle 9.4+ (bundled via wrapper, no manual install needed)

## System Dependencies (Ubuntu 26)

Install the packages needed to cross-compile GMP and MPFR for Android and run
the Gradle build:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y \
    openjdk-17-jdk \
    wget \
    unzip \
    m4 \
    build-essential \
    autoconf \
    automake \
    libtool \
    git
```

### Android SDK and NDK

If you don't already have the Android SDK installed:

```bash
# Download Android command-line tools
wget https://dl.google.com/android/repository/commandlinetools-linux-11076708_latest.zip
mkdir -p ~/Android/Sdk/cmdline-tools
unzip commandlinetools-linux-*.zip -d ~/Android/Sdk/cmdline-tools
mv ~/Android/Sdk/cmdline-tools/cmdline-tools ~/Android/Sdk/cmdline-tools/latest

# Set environment variables (add to ~/.bashrc)
export ANDROID_HOME=~/Android/Sdk
export ANDROID_SDK_ROOT=$ANDROID_HOME
export PATH=$PATH:$ANDROID_HOME/cmdline-tools/latest/bin:$ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools

# Accept licenses
sdkmanager --licenses

# Install required SDK components
sdkmanager "platform-tools" \
           "platforms;android-34" \
           "build-tools;34.0.0" \
           "ndk;27.0.12077973" \
           "cmake;3.22.1"
```

Set `ANDROID_NDK_HOME` for cross-compilation scripts:

```bash
export ANDROID_NDK_HOME=$ANDROID_HOME/ndk/27.0.12077973
```

## Native Dependencies (CGAL, GMP, MPFR, Boost)

The native CGAL compute engine requires the following libraries placed in the
`app/vendor/` directory. These are mandatory — the build will fail without them.

### Directory Layout

```
app/vendor/
├── cgal/include/CGAL/         ← CGAL headers (header-only)
├── boost/include/boost/       ← Boost headers (header-only)
├── gmp/
│   ├── include/gmp.h
│   └── lib/{arm64-v8a,armeabi-v7a,x86_64}/libgmp.a
└── mpfr/
    ├── include/mpfr.h
    └── lib/{arm64-v8a,armeabi-v7a,x86_64}/libmpfr.a
```

### Install CGAL Headers

CGAL is header-only since version 5.0. Minimum required: CGAL 5.6.

```bash
# Ubuntu 26 has CGAL packaged — grab headers from there
sudo apt install -y libcgal-dev

# Copy headers to vendor directory
cp -r /usr/include/CGAL app/vendor/cgal/include/
```

Or download from source:

```bash
wget https://github.com/CGAL/cgal/releases/download/v6.0.1/CGAL-6.0.1-library.tar.xz
tar xf CGAL-6.0.1-library.tar.xz
cp -r CGAL-6.0.1/include/CGAL app/vendor/cgal/include/
```

### Install Boost Headers

Only headers are needed (no compiled Boost libraries). Minimum required: Boost 1.72.

```bash
sudo apt install -y libboost-dev

# Copy headers to vendor directory
cp -r /usr/include/boost app/vendor/boost/include/
```

Or download from source:

```bash
wget https://archives.boost.io/release/1.86.0/source/boost_1_86_0.tar.gz
tar xf boost_1_86_0.tar.gz
cp -r boost_1_86_0/boost app/vendor/boost/include/
```

### Cross-Compile GMP for Android

GMP must be compiled as a static library for each target ABI.

```bash
# Download GMP source
wget https://gmplib.org/download/gmp/gmp-6.3.0.tar.xz
tar xf gmp-6.3.0.tar.xz
cd gmp-6.3.0

export NDK=$ANDROID_NDK_HOME
export TOOLCHAIN=$NDK/toolchains/llvm/prebuilt/linux-x86_64
export API=26

# --- arm64-v8a ---
export TARGET=aarch64-linux-android
export CC=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang
export CXX=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang++

./configure --host=$TARGET --prefix=$(pwd)/install-arm64 \
    --disable-shared --enable-static CC="$CC" CXX="$CXX"
make -j$(nproc) && make install
make distclean

# --- armeabi-v7a ---
export TARGET=armv7a-linux-androideabi
export CC=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang
export CXX=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang++

./configure --host=arm-linux-androideabi --prefix=$(pwd)/install-armv7a \
    --disable-shared --enable-static CC="$CC" CXX="$CXX"
make -j$(nproc) && make install
make distclean

# --- x86_64 ---
export TARGET=x86_64-linux-android
export CC=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang
export CXX=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang++

./configure --host=$TARGET --prefix=$(pwd)/install-x86_64 \
    --disable-shared --enable-static CC="$CC" CXX="$CXX"
make -j$(nproc) && make install

# Copy results to vendor directory
cd ..
cp gmp-6.3.0/install-arm64/include/gmp.h app/vendor/gmp/include/
cp gmp-6.3.0/install-arm64/lib/libgmp.a  app/vendor/gmp/lib/arm64-v8a/
cp gmp-6.3.0/install-armv7a/lib/libgmp.a app/vendor/gmp/lib/armeabi-v7a/
cp gmp-6.3.0/install-x86_64/lib/libgmp.a app/vendor/gmp/lib/x86_64/
```

### Cross-Compile MPFR for Android

MPFR depends on GMP — build GMP first.

```bash
# Download MPFR source
wget https://www.mpfr.org/mpfr-current/mpfr-4.2.1.tar.xz
tar xf mpfr-4.2.1.tar.xz
cd mpfr-4.2.1

export NDK=$ANDROID_NDK_HOME
export TOOLCHAIN=$NDK/toolchains/llvm/prebuilt/linux-x86_64
export API=26

# --- arm64-v8a ---
export TARGET=aarch64-linux-android
export CC=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang
export CXX=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang++

./configure --host=$TARGET --prefix=$(pwd)/install-arm64 \
    --disable-shared --enable-static \
    --with-gmp=$(pwd)/../gmp-6.3.0/install-arm64 \
    CC="$CC" CXX="$CXX"
make -j$(nproc) && make install
make distclean

# --- armeabi-v7a ---
export TARGET=armv7a-linux-androideabi
export CC=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang
export CXX=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang++

./configure --host=arm-linux-androideabi --prefix=$(pwd)/install-armv7a \
    --disable-shared --enable-static \
    --with-gmp=$(pwd)/../gmp-6.3.0/install-armv7a \
    CC="$CC" CXX="$CXX"
make -j$(nproc) && make install
make distclean

# --- x86_64 ---
export TARGET=x86_64-linux-android
export CC=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang
export CXX=$TOOLCHAIN/bin/${TARGET}${API}-clang++

./configure --host=$TARGET --prefix=$(pwd)/install-x86_64 \
    --disable-shared --enable-static \
    --with-gmp=$(pwd)/../gmp-6.3.0/install-x86_64 \
    CC="$CC" CXX="$CXX"
make -j$(nproc) && make install

# Copy results to vendor directory
cd ..
cp mpfr-4.2.1/install-arm64/include/mpfr.h app/vendor/mpfr/include/
cp mpfr-4.2.1/install-arm64/lib/libmpfr.a  app/vendor/mpfr/lib/arm64-v8a/
cp mpfr-4.2.1/install-armv7a/lib/libmpfr.a app/vendor/mpfr/lib/armeabi-v7a/
cp mpfr-4.2.1/install-x86_64/lib/libmpfr.a app/vendor/mpfr/lib/x86_64/
```

### Verify Vendor Dependencies

```bash
find app/vendor -name "*.a" -o -name "*.h" | head -20
ls app/vendor/cgal/include/CGAL/ | head -5
ls app/vendor/boost/include/boost/ | head -5
```

## Building the APK

Once all dependencies are in place:

```bash
# Debug build
./gradlew assembleDebug

# The APK is at:
#   app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk

# Release build (unsigned)
./gradlew assembleRelease
```

## Running Tests

```bash
# Unit tests (Kotlin engine, property tests, serialization)
./gradlew test

# Full build with lint and tests
./gradlew build
```

## Building for a Single ABI

To speed up development builds, restrict to one ABI in `app/build.gradle.kts`:

```kotlin
ndk {
    abiFilters += listOf("arm64-v8a")
}
```

Or pass it via command line:

```bash
./gradlew assembleDebug -Pabi=arm64-v8a
```

## Troubleshooting

### CMake fails with "CGAL headers not found"

You haven't placed the vendor dependencies yet. Follow the "Native Dependencies"
section above. The CMake configuration checks for:

1. `app/vendor/cgal/include/CGAL` directory
2. `app/vendor/boost/include/boost` directory
3. `app/vendor/gmp/lib/<ABI>/libgmp.a` for each configured ABI
4. `app/vendor/mpfr/lib/<ABI>/libmpfr.a` for each configured ABI

### NDK not found

Set `ANDROID_NDK_HOME` or ensure your `local.properties` has the correct
`ndk.dir` path. The project requires NDK r25+ (r27 recommended).

### JDK version mismatch

The project requires JDK 17. Check with `java -version`. On Ubuntu:

```bash
sudo update-alternatives --config java
# Select java-17-openjdk
```


## Performance Testing (Benchmark Suite)

The benchmark module runs OpenSCAD test cases through the Kotlin compute engine,
measures execution time, and writes STL output for correctness verification.

### Running All Benchmarks

```bash
./gradlew :benchmark:cleanTest :benchmark:test --tests '*BenchmarkTest*'
```

This runs all test cases (50+ built-in + custom), prints a timing summary to
stdout, and writes STL files to disk.

### Running Only Property Tests (no mesh generation)

```bash
./gradlew :benchmark:test
```

### Running a Specific Test Class

```bash
# Only the benchmark suite (timing + STL output)
./gradlew :benchmark:test --tests '*BenchmarkTest*'

# Only the test case registry validation
./gradlew :benchmark:test --tests '*TestCaseRegistryTest*'

# Only STL filename sanitization properties
./gradlew :benchmark:test --tests '*StlFileNamerPropertyTest*'
```

### File Locations

| What | Path |
|------|------|
| Source .scad files (built-in) | `benchmark/src/test/resources/testcases/{category}/{name}.scad` |
| Source .scad files (custom) | `benchmark/src/test/resources/testcases/custom/{name}.scad` |
| Expected results (reference STL) | `benchmark/src/test/resources/expected_results/custom_{name}.stl` |
| Generated STL output | `benchmark/build/benchmark-stl/{category}_{name}.stl` |
| Test case registry | `benchmark/src/test/kotlin/com/openscadviewer/benchmark/TestCaseRegistry.kt` |
| Benchmark runner logic | `benchmark/src/test/kotlin/com/openscadviewer/benchmark/BenchmarkRunner.kt` |

### Adding a Custom Test Case

1. Create the .scad file:
   ```
   benchmark/src/test/resources/testcases/custom/my_model.scad
   ```

2. Generate the reference STL using OpenSCAD:
   ```bash
   openscad -o benchmark/src/test/resources/expected_results/custom_my_model.stl \
            benchmark/src/test/resources/testcases/custom/my_model.scad
   ```

3. Register in `TestCaseRegistry.kt`:
   ```kotlin
   val custom: List<TestCase> = listOf(
       TestCase(name = "sun", category = "custom", code = loadCode("custom", "sun")),
       TestCase(name = "my_model", category = "custom", code = loadCode("custom", "my_model"))
   )
   ```

4. Run and compare:
   ```bash
   ./gradlew :benchmark:cleanTest :benchmark:test --tests '*BenchmarkTest*'
   ```

   The output will include a comparison section showing triangle count
   differences between your generated STL and the OpenSCAD reference.

### Output Format

The benchmark prints results grouped by engine:

```
engine - kotlin
prim_cube_unit - 0.001 seconds
  prim_cube_unit.scad -> ./benchmark/build/benchmark-stl/geometry_primitives_prim_cube_unit.stl
sun - 0.002 seconds
  sun.scad -> ./benchmark/build/benchmark-stl/custom_sun.stl
engine - cgal
prim_cube_unit - 0.000 seconds
  SKIPPED

=== Reference STL Comparison ===
sun: 672 triangles (generated) vs 672 (expected) ✓ MATCH
```

### Notes

- The benchmark module is excluded from the default `./gradlew check` task.
  You must run it explicitly with `./gradlew :benchmark:test`.
- Custom tests have no code size limit (built-in tests are capped at 2048 chars).
- The CGAL engine will show SKIPPED unless the native library is loaded.
- If a test hangs, the parser has a 10-second timeout safety limit.

### Running with the CGAL Engine

The CGAL engine performs correct CSG boolean operations (difference, intersection)
but requires the native library `libcgal_engine.so` to be available on the JVM
library path.

To run benchmarks with the CGAL engine enabled:

```bash
# Build the native library for your host platform (Linux x86_64)
# (requires CGAL, GMP, MPFR, Boost headers installed system-wide)
mkdir -p cgal-engine/build/native && cd cgal-engine/build/native
cmake ../../src/main/cpp -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
make -j$(nproc)
cd ../../..

# Run benchmarks with native library on the path
./gradlew :benchmark:cleanTest :benchmark:test --tests '*BenchmarkTest*' \
    -Djava.library.path=cgal-engine/build/native
```

If `libcgal_engine.so` is not found, the CGAL engine reports `isAvailable() = false`
and all its results show as SKIPPED. This is safe — the benchmark always completes.

### Engine Comparison: Kotlin vs CGAL

| Feature | Kotlin Engine | CGAL Engine |
|---------|--------------|-------------|
| Union | Renders all children (correct) | Exact boolean union |
| Difference | Renders all children (no subtraction) | Exact boolean subtraction |
| Intersection | Renders all children (no clipping) | Exact boolean intersection |
| Speed | Fast (~1ms for most models) | Slower (exact arithmetic) |
| Dependencies | Pure JVM, no native libs | Requires CGAL/GMP/MPFR native |
| Use case | Quick preview | Correct STL for 3D printing |

Models using only primitives + transforms + union render identically on both
engines. Models using `difference()` or `intersection()` require the CGAL engine
for correct output.

### Implementing CSG in Pure Kotlin — Cost Estimate

Implementing correct CSG boolean operations in pure Kotlin (eliminating the CGAL
dependency) would require:

**Scope:**
- BSP (Binary Space Partitioning) tree-based CSG algorithm
- Robust polygon splitting with numerical tolerance handling
- Coplanar face detection and classification
- Mesh cleanup (T-junction removal, degenerate triangle filtering)
- Proper handling of edge cases (touching faces, coincident planes)

**Estimated effort:**
- Core BSP tree: ~1500-2000 lines of Kotlin
- Polygon splitting/clipping: ~800-1000 lines
- Numerical robustness layer: ~500 lines
- Mesh cleanup and triangulation: ~500 lines
- Total: ~3000-4000 lines, 2-4 weeks of focused development

**Trade-offs:**
- Floating-point arithmetic (unlike CGAL's exact rationals) means edge cases
  with coplanar or near-coplanar faces may produce artifacts
- Performance likely 5-20x slower than CGAL for complex models
- Simpler models (few boolean ops) would work reliably
- Complex models (nested differences, many intersections) risk numerical instability

**Alternative: use an existing JVM CSG library**
- `jcsg` (Java CSG using BSP): ~ready to integrate, BSD licensed
- Would give correct difference/intersection with moderate robustness
- Not as robust as CGAL for degenerate cases but sufficient for most 3D print models

